1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,000
This is Task 11, Docker.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,000
A Dockerfile is the recipe for an image.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
An image is a read only template that contains our application, Python and all dependencies.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
This is the first half.

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:25,000
It starts from a small official Python image, receives the version and commit as build arguments, and stores them in environment variables and labels.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Then it installs the dependencies from requirements dot txt in their own layer, so Docker can cache that step.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The second half copies the application code, then creates a normal user, so the app does not run as root.

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:58,000
It declares the data folder as a volume, exposes port eight thousand, and defines a health check: every ten seconds Docker calls the health address, and marks the container unhealthy after three failures.

9
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,000
The last line starts Gunicorn, the production web server.

10
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Docker Compose describes the complete application in one file.

11
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,000
I print the important lines.

12
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:22,000
The app service uses the image tagged with the version and commit, gets its settings from environment variables, and mounts the named volume hotel data.

13
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:31,000
The nginx service publishes the public port, waits until the app is healthy, and both restart automatically unless stopped on purpose.

14
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,000
The difference between an image and a container.

15
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:45,000
The images list shows templates that exist on this computer: repository, tag, image id and size.

16
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,000
A container is a running or stopped instance of an image.

17
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:53,000
It has a name, a status and its own writable layer.

18
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Like a class and an object: one image can create many containers.

19
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:04,000
These are the project's existing containers, currently in whatever state they were left.

20
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Now I build an image myself.

21
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Docker build reads the Dockerfile.

22
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:18,000
The dash t option gives it a name and a tag, here hotel booking app, colon demo.

23
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:23,000
The build arguments bake in a demo version and the real commit id.

24
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Then docker tag adds a second name to the same image.

25
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,000
The listing shows both tags with the same image id: a tag is only a label, not a copy.

26
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Now I run a container from that image.

27
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Dash d runs it in the background.

28
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Dash p maps port 18000 on my computer to port 8000 inside the container.

29
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:57,000
The environment variable gives the secret key, and the dash v option mounts a named volume at slash data, where the database lives.

30
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:06,000
After a few seconds Docker shows the container as healthy, and the health address answers with the demo version and the commit.

31
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Docker inspect shows everything about a container.

32
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:22,000
I ask for a few facts: the state is running, the health is healthy, the image it came from, and the restart policy.

33
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
A container started with docker run has no restart policy.

34
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Compose adds unless stopped.

35
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Then the port mapping and the volume: my named volume is mounted at slash data.

36
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Docker logs shows what the application printed.

37
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Here are the last lines: Gunicorn starting its workers, then the access log lines, including the health checks that Docker itself makes every ten seconds.

38
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Logs are the first place to look when a container misbehaves.

39
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:01,000
The lifecycle.

40
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Docker stop sends a polite signal and the container exits.

41
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Its status is Exited.

42
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,000
The container still exists, with its data.

43
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Docker start brings the same container back, and after a few seconds the health check passes again.

44
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Docker restart does both in one command.

45
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Does the data survive?

46
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
Not automatically.

47
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:38,000
It survives only because of the volume.

48
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:48,000
First I write a row into the real SQLite database inside the container, a test user, and read back the number of users: one.

49
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Now I delete the container completely, with remove dash f.

50
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
Its writable layer is gone.

51
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Then I start a brand new container from the same image and the same volume.

52
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
When I count the users again, the row is still there: users, one.

53
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:16,000
The data lives in the volume, not in the container, which is why Compose can replace containers during a deployment without losing bookings.

54
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:30,000
Finally, I remove everything the demo created: the container, the volume and the two demo tags.

55
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Then I list what is left: no demo containers, and the project's real volumes, like the staging and production data and Jenkins, are untouched.


